The MCP default-deny posture is now configuration on the registry app rather than a model default here, and a deployment that does not set it gets the permissive one. `nautobot-ai-models` is a catalogue, and its defaults are a catalogue's: a newly discovered tool arrives enabled, and one whose definition changes stays enabled. Set `new_tools_enabled: False` and `disable_on_definition_change: True` under `nautobot_ai_models` in `PLUGINS_CONFIG` to restore what [ADR 0007](../docs/decisions/0007-mcp-tools-streamable-http-and-default-deny.md) decided. Both are documented in `docs/admin/mcp.md`, set in the development stack, and named in that ADR's amendment.

What does not depend on that configuration is unchanged: a writable tool still needs a human approval per call, and a call whose tool has changed since it was proposed - its definition, its name, or the endpoint it is reached at - is still refused at execution. That last check used to be a backstop behind an automatic disable and is now the thing itself, so it is unconditional rather than guarded.

Registry permissions move with the registry. A role holding `nautobot_event_tracker.change_mcptool` or `add_mcptool` needs the `nautobot_ai_models` equivalents instead. A migration cannot do this: object permissions are deployment data.
